            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="true" heatedGarage="false" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



I was unable to look for air leakage with the infrared camera because the outside temperature was too warm on the day of your testing. Based on the blower door test, your house is not that leaky. However there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage often occurs at the joints between walls, floors, and ceilings, and around window frames. 



I noticed that the chimney for the original furnace location is still on your roof. This may be causing air leakage. Other upgrades discussed in this report, such as adding exterior insulation, removing the heater in your porch, or replacing the window in your bedroom, will also affect your air leakage.



Other upgrades detailed in this report that may reduce air leakage include upgrading your hot water (which will eliminate a chimney), replacing your wood stove with a pellet stove and dedicated combustion air supply will eliminate that chimney. Removing the wall heater near your back door will also eliminate that chimney and reduce air leakage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;12.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, and your back porch appears to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to your entire house.



If you do choose to do this upgrade, I recommend extending the exterior insulation down to ground level to further increase your savings.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $380&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house has 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation and an additional R12 of blown cellulose insulation for a total of R32.  I have modelled adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to bring the total up to R51.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $90&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. Because you already have R32 insulation in your attic, you would not qualify for any rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors>

The back porch of your home is considered an exposed floor. It appears to be 2x10 joists with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. Since you identified that this area of your floor is often cold in the winter, I have modelled increasing the insulation to R32 fibreglass batt insulation and adding 3.0 inches (R15) of rigid XPS insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing the fibreglass batt insulation to R32 and adding an additional R15 of rigid insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $20&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$190 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x4 framing and R12 insulation, with several gaps in this framing. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $380&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of your upstairs windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



I have not modelled the energy upgrades for your basement windows, as these windows are newer and appeared to be in good physical condition at the time of my site visit. 



Since the frame of your bedroom window has been removed, I would recommend starting with that window. 



I have not modelled the energy savings for upgrading your doors, but as your front door is solid wood I recommend replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your upstairs windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, you mentioned some concern about indoor air quality. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



I also recommend installing ENERGY STARÂ® rated bathroom fans in both your bathrooms.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;spend $130&lt;/b&gt; per year in additional energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



COMBUSTION AIR



During my site visit, I saw what appeared to be a combustion air supply leading into a storage room in your basement. Based on the information you provided me,  this may have been left over from when the furnace was moved. I suggest that you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace and hot water tank are satisfied. If this old combusiton air duct is not required, I suggest removing it and sealing the opening in your wall. 



If a combustion air pipe is required, it should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Since you expressed interest in this, I modelled replacing your propane hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your propane fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $13&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current propane fired furnace is rated at 95% AFUE. This is the most energy efficient option available at the moment.



I do suggest that you consider removing the heater in your porch, as you are not using it. Removing the exhaust duct for this heater will reduce your air leakage. 



I also modelled replacing the wood stove in your basement with a direct vent  pellet stove and using for 50% of your heating load in the basement. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your wood stove with a pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



While I have included this in my report, I do 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 750&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
